</div>

<div class="mb-12 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-6" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">What is the first question to ask a healthcare AI supplier?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">Ask what exact job the tool does and what evidence shows it does that job in a setting like yours. A general claim about accuracy is not enough. Ask for the study, who ran it, and which outcome it measured.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">Is regulatory clearance enough to buy?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">No. CIDRAP explains that a US 510(k) clearance means the FDA judged a device substantially equivalent to an existing one, so clearance alone is not an outcomes study. Treat it as one input and still ask for outcome and false-alarm data.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">Why do false alarms matter so much?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">An independent validation of one widely used sepsis model found it did not detect sepsis in 67% of patients with sepsis while alerting on 18% of all patients. High alert volume with low accuracy wastes staff time and can lead people to ignore alerts.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">How long should a pilot run?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">There is no single correct length. The randomized scribe trial summarized by UCLA ran about two months and its authors called for longer studies, so a pilot should run long enough to see error patterns and set review dates before you start.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

